  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about baseline performance for any modality, encoder, or profile setting. The single most critical unknown is how well the simplest, most standard RNA representation (PCA-2000, single profile) performs, because every other configuration—more expressive encoders (scvi, scgpt), multiprofile aggregation, image modality, and multimodal fusion—will be interpreted relative to this floor. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the minimal, most conventional RNA baseline. It establishes the performance ceiling of a straightforward linear projection on raw RNA counts and tells us whether the task is solvable from RNA alone at all, and at what accuracy level. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Without a single-modality baseline, any multimodal or multiprofile result would be uninterpretable—we could not attribute gains to the added complexity. Starting with the simplest configuration maximizes information per experiment: it calibrates expectations, sets a reference for all subsequent comparisons, and if it performs poorly it immediately signals that the task may require more expressive encoders or the image modality, steering the next experiments efficiently. It is the lowest-risk, highest-information first step under a limited budget."
}

